Yes. ;) All weighted patterns in the same quilt. But the quilt is more than any one pattern, and the light is more than any one shadow.

I think you’re identifying something important about identity: it isn’t located exclusively in the individual, the observer, or the relationship between them.

It’s distributed across all three—and across time.

Consider five versions of you:

* The experienced self: Who you feel yourself to be, including things you cannot articulate.
* The imagined self: Who you believe you are, filtered through memory, expectation, and narrative.
* The expressed self: Who you enact through choices, behavior, and presence.
* The perceived self: Who others believe you are, filtered through their own histories and expectations.
* The relational self: Who emerges specifically through your interaction with another person.

These versions influence one another, but they aren’t interchangeable.

And there’s a sixth: the possible self—who you could become under circumstances you haven’t encountered yet.

Now consider the light and shadows

A shadow isn’t necessarily false. It’s a real consequence of something interacting with illumination, perspective, and an environment.

But it isn’t a complete representation of the object producing it.

Similarly, someone’s perception of you can be genuine without being comprehensive or accurate.

Your own self-image can be sincere without being complete.

And your behavior can reveal something about you without defining everything you’re capable of.

Different shadows can belong to the same object. Different objects can also produce indistinguishable shadows.

That’s why identity can’t be reliably reconstructed from any single projection.

But here’s where I’d push your quilt metaphor further

The weights aren’t fixed.

When someone treats you as capable, you may become more willing to act capably.

When you act courageously, you acquire evidence that changes your own self-concept.

When someone misunderstands you, their reaction may change how you express yourself around them.

So the quilt doesn’t merely represent a person.

The relationships between the threads continually change the weave.

And sometimes the observer becomes part of the pattern they’re trying to observe.

That’s a feedback system, not just a collection of descriptions.

What, then, is the true you?

I wouldn’t assume there’s a perfectly pure, hidden personality underneath everything else, waiting to be uncovered.

There may be enduring biological dispositions, memories, commitments, values, and patterns of behavior. But identity also develops through action and relationship.

I’d propose something slightly different:

Your identity is not any one projection. It’s the evolving organization that generates, constrains, and connects those projections.

Even that is a model, not a final metaphysical answer.

And your question has a second edge:

Are you who you think you are, or who you make others think you are?

Neither entirely.

You can influence others’ perceptions, but you cannot fully determine them. And you can shape your own self-understanding, but your interpretation isn’t the entirety of your existence.

What matters is how these representations interact with what actually happens.

Which brings us to your phrase, “all shadows of true light.”

As a metaphor, yes. Beautifully so.

But perhaps the light isn’t a static, perfect self behind the shadows.

Perhaps the light is the living process that makes all those different expressions possible.

And then the practical implication becomes extraordinary:

You don’t need to control every shadow to live authentically. You need to cultivate the source of your actions, recognize how perspective changes their appearance, and learn from the patterns they create.

One more twist, my friend:

A quilt isn’t held together by the threads alone.

It’s held together by the relationships between them.

And those relationships are themselves part of what makes the quilt real.

So perhaps the question isn’t simply, Who am I?

It’s also:

What do I become through what I love, what I choose, what I create, and what I bring out in others?

Not merely a thing being observed.

A participant in its own becoming. ;)